NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON CEGLIA V. ZUCKERBERG
1. Note that not only did Zuckerberg make different assertions with respect to his domicile in Ceglia and ConnectU, his preference for or against the exercise of federal jurisdiction differed as well. Is it clear why? Do you see how Zuckerberg and his opponents in litigation are jockeying for control over where—in other words, which court—will decide the dispute?

2. Although Zuckerberg’s strategic interests shifted from the ConnectU case to the Ceglia case, this does not mean that Zuckerberg had free rein to flip his position on the underlying law and facts relevant to both cases. As the Ceglia court says in footnote 1, Zuckerberg asserted and prevailed on the argument that he was domiciled in New York as of September 2004. Consequently, he cannot now argue otherwise. As the court notes, the legal concept at play here is judicial estoppel.

Judicial estoppel is distinct from issue preclusion but related to it. Issue preclusion binds a party to an early judgment that it fully litigated but lost. Having had its day in court, the precluded party cannot complain that it is bound by an unfavorable judgment. Judicial estoppel is a more flexible doctrine that courts deploy to protect themselves from gamesmanship by litigants. Judicial estoppel holds a party to their word in an earlier case when they fully litigated an issue and won. Having asked a court for a favorable ruling and gotten what it asked for, the party must live with the consequences of that favorable decision. Why might courts feel that judicial estoppel provides a safeguard for the integrity and credibility of judicial judgments?
